kernel: enforce pids.max (upstream 173505fa3)

What: upstream commit 173505fa3 "Fix cgroup pids.max enforcement and
parsing" (master, 2026-11-11; in release-20261116.0), as it is but for the
includes of test/syscalls/linux/cgroup.cc:
- Task.chargeLocked charges the task's cgroup on the hierarchy that has the
  controller charged, instead of the first of its cgroups in Go's map order,
  which the charge then silently missed unless that one held the pids
  controller.
- The parent is charged for a new task under TaskSet.mu, so that it cannot
  move to another cgroup between the charge and the child's entry, and a
  pending charge taken back to 0 is deleted.
- cgroup v1's pids.max compares "max" with the bytes written only, and
  cgroup v2's refuses a value above PID_MAX_LIMIT, as Linux does.

Why: docker run --pids-limit and podman run --pids-limit in a sandbox write
pids.max of the container's cgroup, which did not hold: with --pids-limit 10,
a container of Docker 29.1.3 in a sandbox ran 21 tasks (pids.current 21,
pids.max 10). With patch 0019's freezer there are eight hierarchies, so a
fork was charged to the pids cgroup one time in eight.

Only a cgroup's own pids.max is enforced, not an ancestor's: the Sentry's pids
controller, as upstream, charges and checks the task's own cgroup, not every
ancestor up the hierarchy, so a fork in parent/child can go past parent's
pids.max. Docker and Podman set --pids-limit on the container's own cgroup,
which holds. Linux's kernel/cgroup/pids.c:pids_try_charge() charges the
ancestors too; matching that is not a small change and upstream does not, so
it is left as is.

Upstream: 173505fa3; drop this patch with a release that has it.

Verified: the syscall tests PIDsCgroup.LimitEnforced and SetInvalidLimit,
which the commit adds or extends, pass under gVisor's test runner with the
rest of cgroup_test (64) and cgroup2_test (86, 2 skipped); LimitEnforced
failed on ALE's 0.1.3 without this patch. In sandboxes under runsc-nested,
rootful (Docker 29.1.3, Linux 7.0) and rootless (Docker 29.9.3, Linux 6.12),
a container of Docker 29.1.3 and of Podman 4.9.3 given --pids-limit 10
started 8 of 20 sleeps, each from a shell of its own, as Linux would let
it: the container's shell, a sleep and the shell starting the next fill the
ten.

